  "notes": "The CMG axis's sample-flip board, and the reason modern Memphis has an 80s-R&B harmonic wing. His model is his session order: **sample first, kit behind it.** That gives the most harmonically rich parameter set in this batch and the only one that inherits its harmony from a source record rather than writing it — the flagship flip supplies minor-9 voicings and real ii–v–i motion, so `extensions` runs seventh 0.5 / ninth 0.32 against the lane's 0.07/0.01, `voicing` is `open` across 48–80 with `topVoiceStepwise`, families are 4-chord, and the scale set reaches dorian. Two more follow from the same fact. (1) The kit sits *behind* the loop with transients softened: velocity tiers 54–98, `quantizeStrength` 0.86, percs at −8 dB — the quietest drums here. (2) The 808 follows the sample's bass motion instead of pedalling a root, so `bassline.rhythm` is `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.5 with `glideProb` 0.4 and `portamentoMs` 60–120, the longest portamento in this batch. The drumless 4–8 bar sample intro is authored as an `arrangement.sectionRules` override — the intro carries chords and melody, not drums — and the \"let the beat breathe\" practice is the low drop-out threshold. `timbreHint` is `pitched_vocal_chop` for the chopped-and-pitched vocal hook layer. ⛔ `low` because the research rates it medium-low: the credits, the sample source and the sample-first session practice are sourced, **and every drum value plus the 130–152 straight tempo band are lane inferences**. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 48 to 47 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 48 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
